{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Take now your son, your only one.</b> Because he was the son of the mistress [Sarah], and he alone would be called his seed, He called him his only one (יְחִידְךָ). And the expression comes to magnify the commandment, as it says: Take now your son, the only one, the beloved, Isaac, and offer him up as a burnt offering before Me." "<b>Moriah.</b> This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 22:2) — it is Jerusalem, and so too in Chronicles (II Chronicles 3:1): \"To build the house of YHWH in Jerusalem on Mount Moriah.\" And our Rabbis explained (Ta'anit 16a) that from there instruction (הוֹרָאָה) went forth to the world. And Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 22:2) translated it after the name of the incense, for it contains myrrh (מוֹר) and spices, and if so its meaning would be: to the land that would be Moriah, or that was so called from of old after what was destined to be. And in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 55:7) they said thus: the Rabbis say — to the place where the incense is offered, as it says: \"I will go to the mountain of myrrh\" (Song of Songs 4:6). But the opinion of Onkelos, who said \"אַרְעָא פֻּלְחָנָא\" (the land of worship/service), does not appear to be about the myrrh in the incense, for the expression \"פֻּלְחָנָא\" is not used for a single one of the spices of a particular service, and why did he not say \"לְאַרְעָא דִּקְטֹרֶת בֻּסְמִין\" (to the land of incense of spices)? Rather, his intention is to say: in the land where they would serve Elohim there, and he alludes thereby to what they expounded in Pirkei deRabbi Eliezer (31): they said — with a finger the Holy One, blessed be He, showed our father Abraham the altar. He said to him: This is the altar — it is the altar on which the first man offered, it is the altar on which Cain and Abel offered, it is the altar on which Noah and his sons offered, as it says: \"And Abraham built there the altar\" (Genesis 22:9) — \"an altar\" is not written here, but \"the altar\" — it is the altar on which the ancients offered. Thus far. And he made the name \"Moriah\" from מוֹרָא (fear/awe), for there they would fear Elohim and serve before Him.\n\nBut the correct interpretation according to the plain sense is that it is like \"to the mountain of myrrh and to the hill of frankincense\" (Song of Songs 4:6), for on that mountain they would find myrrh and aloes and cinnamon, as in what they said (Jerusalem Pe'ah 7:3): cinnamon used to grow in the land of Israel, and goats and deer would feed on it. Or it was so called in praise of Israel.\n\nNow here He calls the name of the land \"the land of Moriah,\" but there (II Chronicles 3:1) it appears that the Temple Mount alone is called \"Mount Moriah.\" Perhaps the city was called after that mountain which is within it: the land in which Moriah is — and the mountain alone is called Moriah. And Abraham knew the land but did not know the mountain, and therefore He told him to go to the land of Moriah and He would show him one of the mountains there that was called by this name. And He commanded him to offer him up in that place, for it is the mountain Elohim desired for His dwelling. And He wanted the merit of the Binding (עֲקֵדָה) to abide in the offerings forever, as Abraham said: \"YHWH will see\" (Genesis 22:14). And further, for the sake of His righteousness, He made his effort great and wanted him to do this after a journey of three days, for if he were to do so suddenly in his place, his action would have been in haste and confusion, but when it would be after a journey of days, it was already done with settled mind and deliberation. And thus they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 55:6): Rabbi Akiva says — He tested him indeed, so that they should not say: He stunned him and confused him and he did not know what to do."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:21:43.025851271Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}